</div>
<div class="note small"><strong>Cutting-layout assumption:</strong> sheet grain runs along the 96&quot; direction. Cherry parts are oriented so visible grain runs the expected direction and are not rotated when grain preservation is on. Birch parts may rotate for better yield.</div></section>

<section class="card"><h2>Construction rules in this version</h2><p class="small"><strong>Cabinets may be frameless, face-frame overlay, or face-frame inset on a per-cabinet basis.</strong> Structural carcass joints use pocket screws in the supplied project defaults. Pure drawer and single-door cabinets use a birch carcass; mixed drawer/open-cubby cabinets and open-shelf cabinets use cherry carcasses by default so visible interiors are finished. Face frames are modeled as solid-wood perimeter frames and are reported separately as linear stock. Drawer boxes remain 1/2&quot; birch with bottoms fitted completely between the four walls. Open spaces receive applied backs at the rear. Adjustable shelves are pin-supported; fixed mixed-cubby dividers are pocket-screwed. Countertops attach through top stretchers. No edge banding is included.</p><div class="note small"><strong>Toe-kick/base:</strong> each cabinet gets a separate 2&quot;-high 3/4&quot; birch ladder frame (front rail, rear rail, two side rails), intended to use offcuts. A separate 1/4&quot; cherry skin covers the visible front. The BOM treats the cabinet list as one installed run and makes the cherry skin in the longest pieces that fit the configured sheet length; SketchUp shows one skin segment per cabinet because the presentation model spaces cabinets apart.</div></section>

